WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.725
Memory safety in firmware starts with the
fact that embedded C often uses fixed

2
00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:08.453
buffers, direct hardware access, interrupt
handlers, callbacks,

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:11.093
DMA-capable memory, and tight memory
budgets.

4
00:00:11.173 --> 00:00:13.990
These patterns are normal in embedded
engineering,

5
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.551
but they require disciplined review
because the program is responsible for its own

6
00:00:18.631 --> 00:00:19.399
memory rules.

7
00:00:19.479 --> 00:00:23.575
Bounds checks, size tracking, and explicit
initialization are essential.

8
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:26.408
A buffer sized for a command, frame,
sensor sample,

9
00:00:26.488 --> 00:00:30.328
or status string should be checked before
copy, append, parse, format,

10
00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:31.752
or indexing operations.

11
00:00:31.832 --> 00:00:35.225
Reviewers should be able to see the
capacity, the valid length,

12
00:00:35.305 --> 00:00:38.121
and the rule for what happens when input
does not fit.

13
00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.506
Stack usage, heap use, static allocation,

14
00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:43.210
and memory pools should be chosen
deliberately.

15
00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.170
Some firmware avoids heap allocation after
startup.

16
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.467
Some uses fixed pools.

17
00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.659
Some relies heavily on static storage.

18
00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:53.643
Each policy can be reasonable, but it
should define allocation failure,

19
00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.540
cleanup responsibility, lifetime, and
ownership.

20
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.524
Interrupts, callbacks, DMA, and
concurrency complicate assumptions.

21
00:01:00.604 --> 00:01:03.421
A buffer may be written by hardware, read
by a driver,

22
00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:06.189
forwarded to application code, and later
reused.

23
00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:09.342
The code should make it clear when
hardware owns a buffer,

24
00:01:09.422 --> 00:01:10.702
when the CPU may read it,

25
00:01:10.782 --> 00:01:15.006
and when shared state is protected by
synchronization or design constraints.

26
00:01:15.086 --> 00:01:19.119
Defensive firmware avoids unchecked
copies, unsafe string assumptions,

27
00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:22.207
unvalidated indexes, and ambiguous buffer
ownership.

28
00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:26.768
It also avoids treating successful tests
on the normal path as proof that rare error

29
00:01:26.848 --> 00:01:27.680
paths are safe.

30
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.897
Error handling often uses formatting,
logging, cleanup,

31
00:01:30.977 --> 00:01:33.985
and recovery paths that have their own
memory behavior.

32
00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:37.521
Memory safety review should trace pointer
validity, capacity,

33
00:01:37.601 --> 00:01:41.442
lifetime, initialization, and cleanup
across normal and error paths.

34
00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:45.747
Where possible, support the review with
compiler warnings, static analysis,

35
00:01:45.827 --> 00:01:50.499
stack analysis, tests for boundary cases,
and measurements from target-like builds.

36
00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:54.868
Tools help, but they work best when the
design already makes the memory contract

37
00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:55.460
visible.
